feat: landscape doodle artwork as the texture source

Swaps the portrait doodle for the landscape one supplied by the client.
The old source was 736x1086 and had to repeat several times across a
wide hero, so every seam was a visible break in the field; at 1448x1086
one tile now covers a phone outright and about two thirds of a desktop
hero.

Still `repeat` rather than `cover`. Cover would scale the tile to the
tallest surface and blow the marks up on a phone, where the same
artwork has far less width to spread across — tiling at a fixed width
keeps every mark the same size everywhere and simply repeats further
down a long page. Tile width is clamped 560-1400px, near the artwork's
own proportions at both ends, so the marks read at their drawn size
instead of being shrunk into a denser field.

The asset is flattened onto white, resized to 1400px and encoded as
webp: 134KB against the 1.6MB png. The previous hero-doodle.webp is
removed and nothing references it.

Verified on the home page at 1440 and 390: the layer resolves the new
file, tiles at 1181px and 560px, and paints at 24.1% and 30.3%
coverage with no errors.
